------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Reading
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every physical number is bit-identical between the two trees; the only difference is
that the stale tree does the descriptor work twice. Energy, force norm, virial sum,
parameter count and descriptor state-dict key count all agree exactly, and the fixed
tree's weights load into the stale tree with no missing and no unexpected keys, so no
parameter was ever registered twice in either revision: neither defines
`self.descriptor`, so the duplicate call could not duplicate weights. The cost was pure
recomputation.

Timing (/tmp/les_time.py: 9 GPU training-shaped iterations of forward + loss + Adam
step, median of the last 5, measured 2026-10-06):

    OLD (2 descriptor calls)  median 0.0894 s/step  (min 0.0723, max 0.0928)
    NEW (1 descriptor call)   median 0.0718 s/step  (min 0.0592, max 0.0738)

The medians differ by ~20%; the distributions overlap at the edges, so "about 20% on
the median" is the claim and "strictly faster everywhere" is not.

For reference, the arm that was trained on the stale tree (campaign_water_interface
runs/deepmd-les_sB) recorded 0.1142 s/batch over 225,000 steps in its timing.json,
against 0.0873-0.0880 s/batch for the sea arms at the same step budget. That is
consistent with paying for the duplicate call, but it does not isolate it: that arm
also ran a [240, 240, 240] fitter at rcut 6.0.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Corroboration from the real training run, 2026-10-07
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The arm above was rebuilt as `deepmd-les-cace-sched` (same 1566-atom data, same
225,000-step budget, same timing wrapper) and launched on the fixed tree. Its first
block reported:

    s1: 1696.2 s for 18000 steps (94.2 ms/batch)

which sits at 0.0942 s/batch against the sea arms' 0.0873-0.0880 s/batch for the same
step budget and the same data. Two readings, both worth keeping:

  * The stale tree cost this campaign real time. The legacy `deepmd-les_sB` paid
    0.1142 s/batch where the fixed arm pays 0.0942 on the same data - 17.5% less per
    step. The confound is that the legacy arm also ran rcut 6.0 with a
    [240, 240, 240] fitter, so this is corroboration, not an isolated measurement of
    the duplicate call; the isolated measurement is the 0.0894 -> 0.0718 A/B above,
    which is on 192-atom frames and so is not directly comparable in absolute terms.
  * With the descriptor reused, the LES branch still costs ~7% per step over its own
    short-range sibling (0.0942 against 0.0873-0.0880). That residual is the LES path
    itself - the charge net, the Ewald sum, and the extra autograd graph - and it is
    the honest overhead figure for the long-range channel, not the ~30% the stale
    tree implied.

Both figures include the same validation cadence (disp_freq 450 with numb_btch 50, so
50 extra frames per 450 training steps), so they are comparable to each other.

